# Java Coding Standards

- Prefer explicit `public`, `protected`, or `private` on types and members; do not rely on package-private by omission
- Always use braces `{ }` for all control structures, even single-line blocks
- Opening braces on their own line (Allman)
- Add line breaks between logical groups of code
- `var` is fine for local variables
- Enums are fine
- Do not use streams, lambdas, method references, or records unless the user asks
- Prefer `switch` over a chain of `if` / `else if`
- Prefer `for` / `while` and small methods
- Do not force `this.` for instance members; use it only when a name is shadowed
- Always include a space after type cast operators (e.g. `(int) value`)
- When a method signature or argument list spans multiple lines, place the closing `)` on its own line
- For documentation comments, see `documentation-standards.mdc`

```java
// BAD
if (event.getDistance() < 100) fire(3);
if (mode == 1)
{
    ahead(100);
}
else if (mode == 2)
{
    back(100);
}

// GOOD
if (event.getDistance() < 100)
{
    fire(3.0);
}

switch (mode)
{
    case 1:
        ahead(100);
        break;
    case 2:
        back(100);
        break;
}
```
